*************************************************Very important note**************************************************
The chrome DevTools developers and the Selenium developers are entirely 2 separate entities that neither directly work nor directly communicate with each other.
					The Chrome DevTool developers have established the CDP (Chrome DevTools protocol) that enable users to manually access 
					the UI of the Chrome DevTools in browser and interact with it. They also decided, for this CDP, to write methods that automate this manual process.
					These methods can be used by any automation tool such as Selenium, Appium, PlayWright, and many more.
					
					For example, on the CDP official website, one of the methods that Chrome DevTools developers have written is: setDeviceMetricsOverride()
					So, under the 'Emulation'domain (on the CDP website), this method is listed but in the following format:
					[Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride] // 'Emulation' in this line is a domain in CDP language.
					
So now, the question is, how can various automation tools such as Selenium use this method ?? (Once again, these methods are NOT part of the Selenium library).
	The answer is: Selenium developers have written certain lines of code:
	               where one portion specific to Selenium processes all the arguments inputed, in editor, to that selenium method.
	               where these inputs/arguments are stored in an object of a class that implements the Map interface.
	               
	               
	               and the 2nd portion: invokes that specific CDP method (In this example: setDeviceMetricsOverride() in CDP will be called)
	                                    so that it accesses the browser's Chrome DevTool, navigates to the tab of "Toggle Device Emulation"
	                                    and sets width, height, Device Scale Factor, mobile, etc so that we have a specific mobile/device view.
	                                    This invocation is done by returning 
	                                    a command that contains this MAP object along with the CDP method name.
	                                    So that now, the browser is made ready to start automating on it.
	                                    
	               And the cool things is: these two portions of lines of code are wrapped under a Selenium method that has the EXACT same name 
	               & argument(s) as the one in CPD.
	               So this selenium method name is:  "setDeviceMetricsOverride(arguments)" and the one in CPD is  "setDeviceMetricsOverride(exact same arguments)".
	               
and now, the question is: Under what Selenium class is the method "setDeviceMetricsOverride" included in ? ( A method is written in class right ?) 	               
	 The answer is: "Emulation" (same story...).
	 So, in CPD, "Emulation" is a domain. Selenium developers gave the same EXACT name to their Selenium class.
	 
	 The Selenium line of code is ==========> devTools.send(Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride(arguments go here));
	 if you double click on the Selenium class of "Emulation", in this Emulation.java class file, there will be tons of Selenium methods listed
	 that have the EXACT SAME NAMES of all the CDP methods listed under the 'Emulation' domain on the CDP website.
	 	where, once again each single Selenium method has two portions: 1st is specific to Selenium where it makes a connection with the CDP repo.
	 	                                                                2nd: calls the method in CDP.
	              
	  Note: In====> devTools.send(Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride(arguments go here));
	                the entire argument of .send() that is "Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride()" is called a command/event.

					
	
