     	7* Mocking network speeds: 
     	   The following scenario is one of the MOST COMMON & FUNDEMENTAL issue in test automation, yet it is still
     	   unresolved in most automation projects (Simply due to not integrating Chrome DevTools with the automation
     	   tool of choice which allows the utilization of the feature of: Mocking slower/Faster network responses.
     	   This scenario is as follows:
     	   
     	   At the time of developing and debugging you automation scripts in your editor, you come up with scripts 
     	   that work just fine. However; when you start running your automation build from a continuous integration tool
     	   such as Jenkins or whatever, you notice that some of the testcases are failing/ flaky. This is one of the most
     	   frustrating issues where hours upon hours are spent 'tweaking' the test cases so that they pass. needless to 
     	   say, in general, falkiness is attributed to many factors that are: 1- On the test case design level 2- on whether
     	   you're running your jobs in headless mode (Doing so requires incorporating specific coding style in each testcase),
     	   and much more [Regarding flakiness, I will be writing a lengthy article that's going to be icluded in this github
     	   account]. Nevertheless, one of most common reasons to flakiness is: SLow network speeds.
     	   
     	   Let's explore this further:
     	   Traffic might be too high on your webpage at the time you run your automation scripts. For example,
     	   the off-shore team, are accessing/ working on the webpage while your automation scripts are running at the
     	   same time in your nightly time zone. Therefore, the server is bombarded = slowness = automation scripts are failing when running the Jenkins
     	   job (Even though they have been passing in editor when they were being constructed on your PC)De
     	   
     	   Solution:
     	   Using Chrome DevTools, mock slower network speeds so that you CAN understand what sync issues might be
     	   responsible for failing your scripts. Once this is done, now you can develop your test scripts in your
     	   editor according to that. 
     	   		For example, At night according to your time zone, when running your automation job (And while
     	   		the traffic to the webpage is high as it is worked on by the off-shore team), the web page is 
     	   		taking 5 seconds to load. Hence, the sync issues.
     	   		So when developing test cases in editor, you can mock this slow network speed in your browser
     	   		so that you can utilize synchronization/wait properly. Manually this is done with the 'No Throttling' checkbox
     	   		in Chrome DevTools UI in browser in order to reduce the network speed on the browser. We will see how
     	   		to do that with the help of automation (programmatically).
     	   		
